    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A correct interpretation of the tectonic setting in which the Late Cretaceous-Paleogene sedimentary rocks of the Transylvanian Basin (gravity-flow deposits, reef carbonates, and fluvial-deltaic formations) were deposited is difficult because they are covered by younger formations and deep seismic reflection profiles are still lacking. Classic interpretation of the early history of the Transylvanian Basin is, so far, based exclusively on borehole cores and suggests basement extension and sedimentation in N-S oriented grabens. I studied similar Late Cretaceous-Paleogene deposits exposed in the Iara and Salciua basins on the eastern flank of the Apuseni Mountains. Based on the complex kinematic patterns of folds and faults that indicate a transpressional tectonic setting, I propose that these two basins were formed during syn-thrustng episodes of flexural subsidence, followed by isostatic relaxation. The basins are situated on top of the apusenide basement thrust sheets, that were formed in early Late Cretaceous time in response to the continental collision between the Tisia and Dacia microplates, following the mid-Cretaceous closure of the Transylvanian Ocean. Similarly, the Late Cretaceous-Paleogene 'grabens' in the Transylvanian Basins may be in fact flexural basins formed on top of basement thrust sheets.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
